const e="national-history-day",d=2026,t="Read the whole 44-page Contest Rule Book (June 22, 2020 edition) and the nhd.org pages Contest Rules & Evaluation, National Contest, Theme, Get Started and Find Your Local Contest. The Rule Book is still the current edition per nhd.org, so it is the same edition as the template cites. The Rule Book prints no entry fees, no deadlines and no local contest dates: those are set by each Affiliate Contest Coordinator, so the page has a short pointer (Variable LocalContestDates) and no invented dates. The Rule Book prints no points, grade bands or numeric scores: judges rank entries by consensus (rule 7.4). The National Contest dates (June 13-17, 2027), the awards ceremony and the 2027 theme come from nhd.org and are Variables. Not captured: sponsor and donor names and the acknowledgements, the sample figures, the 2025-2026 winners and Teacher of the Year names on nhd.org, and the names and phone numbers of individual Affiliate coordinators (they are on nhd.org/affiliates).",o=JSON.parse(`{"Title":"Rules & Criteria","Subtitle":"National History Day contest rules for students and teachers. Read the Contest Rule Book before you start your project.","NoticeTitle":"Please Read Before Registering","Notice":"Rules for all NHD contests from the Regional to the National level are in the Contest Rule Book ({{RuleBookEdition}}). Your teacher may adapt rules for school-level contests. Dates, deadlines and fees are set by your Affiliate: {{LocalContestDates}}","Notes":["NHD programs are open to all students and teachers without regard to race, religion, physical abilities, economic status, gender or sexual orientation. NHD staff and coordinators try to help students with disabilities.","The 2027 annual theme is {{AnnualTheme}}.","NHD does not censor entries on content at any level. The views in student entries are those of the students and their sources, not of NHD, its donors, sponsors, supporters, partners or Affiliates."],"Sections":[{"Key":"general","Title":"About the Contest and Your Project","Items":["National History Day (NHD), started in 1974, is a non-profit education organisation in College Park, Maryland. It runs a yearlong academic program for middle and high school students. More than 600,000 students around the world research a historical topic each year.","You research a historical topic that connects to the annual theme and show your work in one of five creative categories: Paper, Performance, Documentary, Exhibit or Website. Then you may enter the local NHD contest that serves you.","Each NHD contest has a Contest Coordinator. Affiliates (named by place, for example a state, territory or country) have Affiliate Contest Coordinators. Most Affiliates have Regions with their own contests. Top projects from each Affiliate Contest go to the National Contest at the University of Maryland, College Park.","The program is flexible at the school level. Your teacher may adapt rules or add requirements for school-level contests. The Contest Rule Book rules apply to all contests from the Regional to the National level.","Annual theme: your entry must clearly relate to the annual theme. The theme for 2027 is {{AnnualTheme}}.","Topic: choose a topic that is significant in history, connects to the theme and interests you. It should be narrow enough to research deeply. It needs many sources by historians and sources from the time. Current events are not good topics, because historians cannot yet judge them.","Essential elements of a project: historical context, multiple perspectives (show more than one side), historical accuracy, historical significance (the impact or result of your topic) and a historical argument (the central point in two or three sentences, in the project, labelling it is not required).","Research: start with secondary sources by credible authors such as professional historians, then use primary sources made at the time (eyewitness accounts, letters, speeches, diaries, newspapers, oral histories, images, maps, artifacts, unedited copies from sites such as the National Archives or the Library of Congress). Use a wide variety of sources. Quality counts more than the number.","Oral history is an interview with a person who took part in the event. It is a primary source. An interview with an expert who was not involved is secondary. Interviews with experts are not required. Do not interview a professional historian. Read the work instead.","Primary material found in a secondary source: if it is complete and unedited, cite it as a primary source. If it is a part or a quote, cite it as a source within a source in the secondary section. Explain the use in your annotation.","Student voice: your argument and analysis must be clear in the project. Do not let quotes drown your voice.","Academic integrity: the project must be your own research, analysis and design, and you must credit all sources."]},{"Key":"eligibility","Title":"Who May Enter","Items":["The contest has two divisions by grade: Junior Division is grades 6, 7 and 8. Senior Division is grades 9, 10, 11 and 12.","Some Affiliates also sponsor fourth and fifth graders. Ask your Affiliate Contest Coordinator. That is not counted in the seven-year maximum.","In a nongraded school, ask your Affiliate Contest Coordinator which division you should register in.","If you attend an online school, you must take part in the Affiliate where you live. Ask your Affiliate Contest Coordinator for your Regional Contest.","Students may take part in the Junior and Senior Divisions for a maximum of seven years.","Contest year: it begins right after the National Contest awards ceremony and runs to the next year's National Contest awards ceremony. You may begin work after the previous contest year ends in June.","You may enter only one project in one category in a contest year.","You must do the research, design and creation of your project on your own during the current contest year. You must not share your research with students outside your group, submit someone else's work as your own, or reuse a project from a previous year. You must choose a new topic each year. If you are unsure whether your topic is too like a previous year's, ask your teacher and Contest Coordinator.","Reasonable help is allowed. Examples: a teacher guides your research, teaches you the editing software, reviews your work and suggests edits; a parent cuts your board or prop, or helps to sew a costume that you design; non-team members carry your exhibit or equipment into the contest space; a trusted adult is copied on interview emails and goes with you to the interview. You do the research, make the designs and run the software, set up your exhibit, and make the formal interview request. Objects made by others for your entry break this rule (for example a parent editing your documentary or an artist drawing your backdrop)."]},{"Key":"registration","Title":"How to Enter","Items":["Each Contest Coordinator plans the event and sets the registration procedures, deadlines, contest fees and contest policies. Check your Affiliate website often. Find it at {{AffiliateFinder}}.","At each level you must register, meet the deadlines and follow the procedures of the Contest Coordinator. You are responsible for knowing and meeting the deadlines.","Ask your Affiliate Contest Coordinator for the rules on how many entries go from the school level to the Regional or Affiliate contests.","Written materials and papers must be submitted before the contest. Ask your Contest Coordinator for the submission instructions, deadlines and number of copies.","Websites: send the website URL to the Contest Coordinator before the deadline. Websites are locked during judging. You cannot edit your site from the submission deadline to the end of the contest.","For questions about NHD rules and procedures write to {{NHDContact}}."]},{"Key":"levels","Title":"Rounds and Qualification","Items":["Contest levels: school, school district, Regional and Affiliate. In most Affiliates you qualify for the Affiliate Contest by winning a Regional Contest first. A few Affiliate Contests have open enrolment.","Each Affiliate may send two entries per contest division and category to the National Contest. Ties at Affiliate Contests must be settled at that level.","Entries in each division and category are judged separately at all levels. For example Junior Individual Exhibit, Junior Group Exhibit, Senior Individual Exhibit and Senior Group Exhibit are each judged separately.","National Contest ({{NationalContestDates}}, {{NationalVenue}}): students compete over several days in preliminary and final rounds. A student with an individual entry must be present for the entry to be judged. All members of a group must register for the National Contest, but not all must attend.","Changing categories or topics is not allowed after entering the first level beyond the school. Narrowing and refining a topic is allowed and encouraged. Between contest levels you may continue research and revise your project, including its title, but you may not change your topic."]},{"Key":"dates","Title":"Dates","Items":["National Contest: {{NationalContestDates}}. Awards Ceremony: {{NationalAwardsCeremony}}.","Local, regional and affiliate contest dates, deadlines and fees: {{LocalContestDates}}"]},{"Key":"venue","Title":"Venue","Items":["The National Contest is at {{NationalVenue}}.","You are solely responsible for the safety of your own belongings at every NHD contest. NHD officials and sponsors are not responsible for the loss of or damage to exhibits, props, equipment, other project parts or personal belongings.","You must supply all props and equipment at every level, including computers and software, unless the Contest Coordinator says certain equipment (for example projection screens for documentaries) is provided. Think of transport, set-up time, size and weight. Check with your Contest Coordinator."]},{"Key":"fees","Title":"Fees","Items":["Contest fees are set by each Contest Coordinator. The Contest Rule Book prints no fee amount."]},{"Key":"judging","Title":"Judging","Items":["Judges are historians, educators and others interested in history and education. At Regional, Affiliate and National contests each entry is judged inside its division and category by at least one team of judges. With many entries, several first-round teams are followed by a final round. The number of entries in the finals and the procedures differ by contest and category.","Judges use NHD Evaluation Forms. The form has two sections: Historical Quality (80 percent) and Clarity of Presentation (20 percent).","Historical Quality is the same for all categories. It looks at the strength of the historical argument, the research and the relation to the theme.","Clarity of Presentation is different for each category. It looks at how well the project communicates its argument with the tools of the category. The forms and checklists are at nhd.org/categories.","Judges do not give a numerical score. They work together, discuss each entry and rank the entries in their group (consensus judging).","Judges check objective aspects (for example whether primary sources are used, and grammar and spelling) and subjective aspects (analysis and conclusions). Base your conclusions on solid research.","Be ready to answer judges' questions about the content and development of the entry. You may not give a formal prepared introduction, narration or conclusion in the interview. The entry should stand on its own.","Rule infractions count against the entry, and judges consider them in the final ranking. They are not grounds for disqualification unless they are one of the disqualification reasons (see Conduct)."]},{"Key":"prizes","Title":"Rewards and Awards","Items":["The main reward is the skills and knowledge you gain. At each level, outstanding work may also get certificates, medals, trophies, special topic-related prizes, scholarships or money.","National Contest: the top three entries in each contest category receive awards. There are special prizes, and Outstanding Affiliate Awards (two entries from each Affiliate, one Junior and one Senior)."]},{"Key":"conduct","Title":"Conduct and Disqualification","Items":["The Contest Coordinator can disqualify an entry for these reasons of academic integrity: entering your project in many contests or categories in one contest year, reusing a project or research from a previous year (alone or as a group), plagiarism, and tampering with or removing any part of another entry during a competition.","Plagiarism: using any part of another person's work or ideas without credit. It includes handing in someone else's project, failing to use quotation marks and credits for direct quotes, failing to cite quoted and paraphrased material, failing to name the sources of audio, video or images, and giving wrong information about the source of a quote. It means disqualification.","Tampering: do not tamper with another student's entry. Defacing another student's project on purpose means disqualification. It can be editing or deleting another student's website, defacing or stealing parts of an exhibit, or causing a disturbance during a performance to distract the performer.","Language: all entries must be in English unless the Contest Coordinator approves otherwise. Other languages may be used if English translations are given. For word counts judges count only the English translation.","Copyright: the Fair Use doctrine allows educational use of copyrighted material for non-commercial purposes. Do not put your project in a non-academic public place, such as a commercial internet site, without the permission of the copyright owner.","Costumes: you may not wear costumes related to the focus of your entry during judging, except in the Performance category.","Prohibited materials in any competition venue: weapons of any kind (real, toys or replicas), live animals except service animals, live cultures or organisms, and anything that could damage the venue. In general, if you cannot bring it to school, you cannot bring it to an NHD contest. If you are unsure, ask your Contest Coordinator.","Group entries: after the project enters the first level of competition beyond the school, students may not be added or replaced. If a group loses members, even for a while, the rest may still compete, even a single student. It is still a group entry."]},{"Key":"complaints","Title":"Concerns","Items":["The decision of the judges is final. Judges alone decide each entry's ranking and whether it advances. If you have a concern, ask your teacher about the right way to contact the Contest Coordinator."]},{"Key":"other","Title":"Written Materials for All Categories","Items":["Every entry needs these written materials in this order: a title page, a process paper and an annotated bibliography. Print on plain white 8.5 x 11 inch paper (A4 is accepted from international Affiliates), with 1 inch margins on all sides, in 12 point font. Print on one or both sides. Staple in the top left corner. Do not use a binder or folder.","Exhibit, Performance and Documentary: give the required written materials to the judges. Bring extra copies. Paper: include them with the paper. Website: include them in the website.","Title: the entry must have a title that is clearly seen on the title page and on the project itself.","Title page: the first page of the written materials. It has only the title, your name(s), the contest division and category, and the word counts. It is not in the word count.","Process paper: 500 words or fewer, with no quotes, images or captions. Its words are counted separately. It answers five questions: how you chose your topic and how it relates to the theme, how you did your research, how you made your project, what your historical argument is, and in what ways your topic is significant in history.","Annotated bibliography: list all sources you used. Split it into a primary sources section and a secondary sources section. Combine photos or other material from one collection in a single citation. Do not attach sources. It is not in the word count. Each citation has a short annotation of at most two or three sentences that says how you used the source and how it helped you understand the topic. Explain the reasons for classing any source that is not clearly primary or secondary. A source may not be both.","Style guides: citations follow the latest edition of the Chicago Manual of Style or the MLA Handbook. Keep one style throughout all written material.","Word counting: a date counts as one word, a number counts as one word, words such as a, the and of count one each, each word in a name counts, a hyphenated word counts as one. Words in the title page, process paper and annotated bibliography do not count toward project word counts. The process paper words count only toward its own 500-word limit.","NHD materials: sample entries, instructional videos, category tips and project checklists are at nhd.org and nhd.org/categories. Checklists are not handed in at the contest."]}],"Documents":[{"Title":"Contest Rule Book (June 22, 2020 edition, PDF)","Url":"https://nhd.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/NHDRuleBook2020Digital.pdf"},{"Title":"Contest Rules and Evaluation","Url":"https://nhd.org/en/contest/contest-rules/"},{"Title":"Find your local contest (Affiliate)","Url":"https://nhd.org/en/contest/find-local-contest/"},{"Title":"Annual theme","Url":"https://nhd.org/en/contest/theme/"},{"Title":"National Contest","Url":"https://nhd.org/en/contest/national-contest/"}]}`),n=[{ParticipantType:"Individual",Source:"NHD Contest Rule Book (June 2020 edition) p.7 section 2.3; p.16 Rule 3",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["An individual project must be the work of only one student.","A Paper is individual only. Documentary, Exhibit, Performance and Website have an individual option."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- An individual project must be the work of only one student.
- A Paper is individual only. Documentary, Exhibit, Performance and Website have an individual option.`},{ParticipantType:"Team",Source:"NHD Contest Rule Book (June 2020 edition) p.7 section 2.3; p.8 section 3.4; p.16 Rule 3",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["A group project is the work of two to five students. Group members do not have to be in the same grade, but all must be in sixth grade or above.","A group project enters the division of its oldest member.","All students in a group entry must take part in the research and interpretation of the group's topic.","Once a group project enters the first level of competition beyond the school, students may not be added or replaced. If the group loses one or more members, even for a while, the rest are still eligible to compete, even if only one student is left. It is still a group entry.","At the National Contest all members of a group must register. Not all have to attend.","The team must do the research together and must not share it with students outside the group."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- A group project is the work of two to five students. Group members do not have to be in the same grade, but all must be in sixth grade or above.
- A group project enters the division of its oldest member.
- All students in a group entry must take part in the research and interpretation of the group's topic.
- Once a group project enters the first level of competition beyond the school, students may not be added or replaced. If the group loses one or more members, even for a while, the rest are still eligible to compete, even if only one student is left. It is still a group entry.
- At the National Contest all members of a group must register. Not all have to attend.
- The team must do the research together and must not share it with students outside the group.`}],a=[{AgeGroup:"Junior Division",Source:"NHD Contest Rule Book (June 2020 edition) p.6 section 2.2",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Junior Division is for grades 6, 7 and 8.","Some Affiliates also sponsor fourth and fifth graders. Ask your Affiliate Contest Coordinator. They do not compete in the National Contest.","In a nongraded school, ask your Affiliate Contest Coordinator which division to register in."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Junior Division is for grades 6, 7 and 8.
- Some Affiliates also sponsor fourth and fifth graders. Ask your Affiliate Contest Coordinator. They do not compete in the National Contest.
- In a nongraded school, ask your Affiliate Contest Coordinator which division to register in.`},{AgeGroup:"Senior Division",Source:"NHD Contest Rule Book (June 2020 edition) p.6 section 2.2",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Senior Division is for grades 9, 10, 11 and 12.","In a nongraded school, ask your Affiliate Contest Coordinator which division to register in."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Senior Division is for grades 9, 10, 11 and 12.
- In a nongraded school, ask your Affiliate Contest Coordinator which division to register in.`}],i=JSON.parse(`[{"Title":"Paper","Source":"NHD Contest Rule Book (June 2020 edition) p.24-26 section 6.1 (Paper rules A1-A5)","Sections":{"About":["A paper is a written format for your historical argument, research and interpretation of your topic's significance in history. It is for individual entries only."],"TimeAndFormat":["Length: between 1,500 and 2,500 words."],"Rules":["The paper must be your original work, show your own historical research and analysis, be grammatically correct and follow all general and category rules.","Each word or number in the text counts as one word. Words that count: text you write (including captions) and words in footnotes, endnotes or internal documentation other than the citation, and quotes from any sources. Words that do not count: title page, process paper, annotated bibliography, footnote, endnote or internal documentation citations, and primary source materials in appendices.","Relevant images, maps, graphs or primary source materials must be placed in appendices, mentioned in the text and cited in the annotated bibliography. Use of appendices must be very limited.","Use footnotes, endnotes or internal documentation for citations, in the style of the Chicago Manual of Style or the MLA Handbook.","Print on plain white 8.5 x 11 inch paper (A4 accepted from international Affiliates), double-sided, in 12 point font, with 1 inch margins on all sides. Number the pages in order and double-space.","Combine the title page, process paper, paper and annotated bibliography in this order. Staple in the upper left corner or secure with a clip. Do not use a report cover, folder or binder.","Submit the paper and the written materials before the contest. Ask your Contest Coordinator for the instructions, deadlines and number of copies."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A paper is a written format for your historical argument, research and interpretation of your topic's significance in history. It is for individual entries only.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Length: between 1,500 and 2,500 words.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- The paper must be your original work, show your own historical research and analysis, be grammatically correct and follow all general and category rules.\\n- Each word or number in the text counts as one word. Words that count: text you write (including captions) and words in footnotes, endnotes or internal documentation other than the citation, and quotes from any sources. Words that do not count: title page, process paper, annotated bibliography, footnote, endnote or internal documentation citations, and primary source materials in appendices.\\n- Relevant images, maps, graphs or primary source materials must be placed in appendices, mentioned in the text and cited in the annotated bibliography. Use of appendices must be very limited.\\n- Use footnotes, endnotes or internal documentation for citations, in the style of the Chicago Manual of Style or the MLA Handbook.\\n- Print on plain white 8.5 x 11 inch paper (A4 accepted from international Affiliates), double-sided, in 12 point font, with 1 inch margins on all sides. Number the pages in order and double-space.\\n- Combine the title page, process paper, paper and annotated bibliography in this order. Staple in the upper left corner or secure with a clip. Do not use a report cover, folder or binder.\\n- Submit the paper and the written materials before the contest. Ask your Contest Coordinator for the instructions, deadlines and number of copies."},{"Title":"Performance","Source":"NHD Contest Rule Book (June 2020 edition) p.27-28 section 6.2 (Performance rules B1-B7)","Sections":{"About":["A performance is a dramatic portrayal of your historical argument, research and interpretation of your topic's significance in history. It has individual and group options."],"TimeAndFormat":["The performance may not be longer than 10 minutes. Timing starts at the start of the performance after the introduction.","You have 5 minutes to set up before the performance.","You have 5 minutes after the performance to remove props and take part in an interview with the judges.","Allow several empty seconds in your performance for unplanned pauses (such as audience reaction or forgotten lines)."],"Rules":["The performance must be an original production, presented live, scripted from your research, and should have dramatic appeal.","Before the performance state only your title and name(s).","You must provide and run all equipment, including extension cords, and carry out any special lighting or sound effects. Only you and your group members may be involved in making any media in your performance.","Scripts must not be given to judges or attached to the written materials.","You may have help to make your costume or props, but the design and the choice of fabrics must be your own. Or you may buy or rent a costume.","Give your required written materials (title page, process paper, annotated bibliography) to the judges. Bring extra copies. Ask your Contest Coordinator for submission instructions."],"Allowed":["Projection, mp3 players, computers or other media in the performance.","Costumes that relate to the focus of the entry (the only category where this is allowed)."],"NotAllowed":["Interaction with the audience or requiring the audience to take part."],"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A performance is a dramatic portrayal of your historical argument, research and interpretation of your topic's significance in history. It has individual and group options.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- The performance may not be longer than 10 minutes. Timing starts at the start of the performance after the introduction.\\n- You have 5 minutes to set up before the performance.\\n- You have 5 minutes after the performance to remove props and take part in an interview with the judges.\\n- Allow several empty seconds in your performance for unplanned pauses (such as audience reaction or forgotten lines).\\n\\n## Rules\\n- The performance must be an original production, presented live, scripted from your research, and should have dramatic appeal.\\n- Before the performance state only your title and name(s).\\n- You must provide and run all equipment, including extension cords, and carry out any special lighting or sound effects. Only you and your group members may be involved in making any media in your performance.\\n- Scripts must not be given to judges or attached to the written materials.\\n- You may have help to make your costume or props, but the design and the choice of fabrics must be your own. Or you may buy or rent a costume.\\n- Give your required written materials (title page, process paper, annotated bibliography) to the judges. Bring extra copies. Ask your Contest Coordinator for submission instructions.\\n\\n## Allowed\\n- Projection, mp3 players, computers or other media in the performance.\\n- Costumes that relate to the focus of the entry (the only category where this is allowed).\\n\\n## Not allowed\\n- Interaction with the audience or requiring the audience to take part."},{"Title":"Documentary","Source":"NHD Contest Rule Book (June 2020 edition) p.29-31 section 6.3 (Documentary rules C1-C7)","Sections":{"About":["A documentary is an audio and visual presentation that uses many kinds of sources (images, video, sound) to communicate your historical argument, research and interpretation of your topic's significance in history. It has individual and group options."],"TimeAndFormat":["A documentary may not be longer than 10 minutes.","Timing begins when the first visual image appears or the first sound is heard, and does not include time to test sound or video.","Timing ends when the last image or sound ends, including source credits.","You have up to 5 minutes to prepare the documentary, adjust the volume and so on.","You have 5 minutes after the documentary to remove your equipment and take part in an interview with the judges."],"Rules":["The documentary must be an original production. You must have access to equipment and be able to operate it.","You must announce only the title of the documentary and your name(s) before it starts. No other comments before or during the presentation, including live narration.","You must be able to provide, open and run the file. Once the documentary begins it must run on its own. No interaction with judges or audience.","Some contests ask you to bring your own equipment. Ask your Contest Coordinator. Internet access may not be available. Judges may not run any equipment.","You may be given reasonable help with technical problems beyond your control. Different equipment may change how your documentary looks, so bring backup copies in different formats or your own equipment. You are not penalised for showing it on a laptop.","You must create and produce the entry and operate all equipment, including recording and editing equipment. Only you may write and narrate the script. Only you and the persons you interview may appear in it.","You may use professional photographs, film, recorded music and similar, with source credits.","The last part of the documentary must be a list of acknowledgements and credits for the sources of moving footage, interviews, music and images. The credits are brief (not full citations, not annotated). Items from one collection may be one credit. The credits are inside the 10-minute limit and must be readable.","All sources used (music, images, film or media clips, interviews, books, websites) must be cited in the annotated bibliography.","Give your required written materials to the judges. Bring extra copies. Ask your Contest Coordinator for submission instructions."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":["Materials made by others specifically for use in your entry.","Additional comments or live narration before or during the documentary."],"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A documentary is an audio and visual presentation that uses many kinds of sources (images, video, sound) to communicate your historical argument, research and interpretation of your topic's significance in history. It has individual and group options.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- A documentary may not be longer than 10 minutes.\\n- Timing begins when the first visual image appears or the first sound is heard, and does not include time to test sound or video.\\n- Timing ends when the last image or sound ends, including source credits.\\n- You have up to 5 minutes to prepare the documentary, adjust the volume and so on.\\n- You have 5 minutes after the documentary to remove your equipment and take part in an interview with the judges.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- The documentary must be an original production. You must have access to equipment and be able to operate it.\\n- You must announce only the title of the documentary and your name(s) before it starts. No other comments before or during the presentation, including live narration.\\n- You must be able to provide, open and run the file. Once the documentary begins it must run on its own. No interaction with judges or audience.\\n- Some contests ask you to bring your own equipment. Ask your Contest Coordinator. Internet access may not be available. Judges may not run any equipment.\\n- You may be given reasonable help with technical problems beyond your control. Different equipment may change how your documentary looks, so bring backup copies in different formats or your own equipment. You are not penalised for showing it on a laptop.\\n- You must create and produce the entry and operate all equipment, including recording and editing equipment. Only you may write and narrate the script. Only you and the persons you interview may appear in it.\\n- You may use professional photographs, film, recorded music and similar, with source credits.\\n- The last part of the documentary must be a list of acknowledgements and credits for the sources of moving footage, interviews, music and images. The credits are brief (not full citations, not annotated). Items from one collection may be one credit. The credits are inside the 10-minute limit and must be readable.\\n- All sources used (music, images, film or media clips, interviews, books, websites) must be cited in the annotated bibliography.\\n- Give your required written materials to the judges. Bring extra copies. Ask your Contest Coordinator for submission instructions.\\n\\n## Not allowed\\n- Materials made by others specifically for use in your entry.\\n- Additional comments or live narration before or during the documentary."},{"Title":"Exhibit","Source":"NHD Contest Rule Book (June 2020 edition) p.32-34 section 6.4 (Exhibit rules D1-D6)","Sections":{"About":["An exhibit is a three-dimensional physical and visual representation of your historical argument, research and interpretation of your topic's significance in history. It has individual and group options."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Your analysis must be clear and evident in the exhibit itself. Do not rely on extra material or media devices to deliver your message, as judges may not have time for them.","Support your argument with visual and textual evidence, but do not let it overwhelm the argument.","Size: the exhibit, when displayed for judging, must be no larger than 40 inches wide, 30 inches deep and 72 inches high. Circular or rotating exhibits, or those to be seen from all sides, must be no more than 30 inches in diameter or diagonal. The table is not measured. Any base you make, table drapes you bring and extra materials on the table are measured.","Word limit: 500 words for all words that you write. Each word or number counts as one word. Words that count: text you write, including titles, subtitles, captions, graphs, timelines, media devices and supplemental materials (such as photo albums and scrapbooks). Words that do not count: primary or secondary materials or quotes on the exhibit or in media or supplemental materials, and source credits.","Rely on your own analysis, primary sources and visuals. Be careful about the length and number of quotes. Extensive supplemental material is not appropriate. Oral history transcripts, letters between you and interview subjects, questionnaires and other source materials are cited in the bibliography, not attached to the bibliography or the exhibit.","Media devices or electronics in an exhibit must not run for more than 2 minutes in total and must not loop. They must not have dramatic or narrative student involvement. Judges must be able to control them with clearly visible on/off and volume controls. They must fit the size and word limits of the exhibit.","All quotes and visual sources (photographs, paintings, charts, graphs) must be credited on the exhibit and cited in the annotated bibliography. Source credits do not count toward the word total.","Give your required written materials to the judges. Bring extra copies. Ask your Contest Coordinator for submission instructions."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":["Takeaway items for judges or others.","Anything that links to outside content, such as QR codes."],"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- An exhibit is a three-dimensional physical and visual representation of your historical argument, research and interpretation of your topic's significance in history. It has individual and group options.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Your analysis must be clear and evident in the exhibit itself. Do not rely on extra material or media devices to deliver your message, as judges may not have time for them.\\n- Support your argument with visual and textual evidence, but do not let it overwhelm the argument.\\n- Size: the exhibit, when displayed for judging, must be no larger than 40 inches wide, 30 inches deep and 72 inches high. Circular or rotating exhibits, or those to be seen from all sides, must be no more than 30 inches in diameter or diagonal. The table is not measured. Any base you make, table drapes you bring and extra materials on the table are measured.\\n- Word limit: 500 words for all words that you write. Each word or number counts as one word. Words that count: text you write, including titles, subtitles, captions, graphs, timelines, media devices and supplemental materials (such as photo albums and scrapbooks). Words that do not count: primary or secondary materials or quotes on the exhibit or in media or supplemental materials, and source credits.\\n- Rely on your own analysis, primary sources and visuals. Be careful about the length and number of quotes. Extensive supplemental material is not appropriate. Oral history transcripts, letters between you and interview subjects, questionnaires and other source materials are cited in the bibliography, not attached to the bibliography or the exhibit.\\n- Media devices or electronics in an exhibit must not run for more than 2 minutes in total and must not loop. They must not have dramatic or narrative student involvement. Judges must be able to control them with clearly visible on/off and volume controls. They must fit the size and word limits of the exhibit.\\n- All quotes and visual sources (photographs, paintings, charts, graphs) must be credited on the exhibit and cited in the annotated bibliography. Source credits do not count toward the word total.\\n- Give your required written materials to the judges. Bring extra copies. Ask your Contest Coordinator for submission instructions.\\n\\n## Not allowed\\n- Takeaway items for judges or others.\\n- Anything that links to outside content, such as QR codes."},{"Title":"Website","Source":"NHD Contest Rule Book (June 2020 edition) p.35-38 section 6.5 (Website rules E1-E10)","Sections":{"About":["A website is a set of connected web pages that uses multimedia to communicate your historical argument, research and interpretation of your topic's significance in history. It has individual and group options."],"TimeAndFormat":["Multimedia clips (audio, video or both) may total no more than 3 minutes. Music or songs that play after a page loads are counted in the 3 minutes."],"Rules":["The website must be an original production and show your ability to use website design software and technology. You need access to the internet and appropriate software and equipment.","You must build the entry with the NHD website editor, NHDWebCentral (nhd.org/nhdwebcentral). You must run all software and equipment in developing the site. All pages must be connected by navigation links. The site is limited to 100MB of file space.","You may use professional photographs, graphics, video, recorded music and similar, built into the website, credited in the site and cited in the annotated bibliography.","Word limit: no more than 1,200 visible words that you write. Words that count: text you write, including captions, graphs, timelines and multimedia. Words that do not count: code and alternate text tags on images, required source credits, recurring menus, titles and navigation instructions, primary or secondary materials or quotes, the required home page items, and the annotated bibliography and process paper.","Home page: one page must be the home page. It shows your name(s), the entry title, division and category, the number of visible student-written words, the total length of multimedia, the number of words in the process paper, and the main menu to the sections. It must not have the name of your teacher or your school.","You may record quotes and primary source materials for dramatic effect, but you may not narrate your own compositions or other explanatory material.","If multimedia needs special software to view (for example Flash, QuickTime or Real Player), give a link on the same page to a website where it is a free, secure and legal download.","Judges try to view all multimedia, but files that cannot be viewed cannot be judged.","All quotes and visual sources must be credited on the website and cited in the annotated bibliography. Remove the hyperlink from all URLs in the on-screen source credit. Source credits do not count toward the word total.","The annotated bibliography and process paper must be part of the website, in PDF format and in the navigation. No printed copies are needed.","Extensive supplemental material is not appropriate. Oral history transcripts, letters, questionnaires and other sources are cited in the bibliography, not added to the website.","The content and look of a page cannot change when the page is refreshed. Random text or image generators are not allowed.","The pages must be viewable in a recent version of a standard web browser (for example Microsoft Edge, Mozilla Firefox, Apple Safari, Google Chrome).","Send the website URL to the Contest Coordinator by the deadline. The website is locked during judging."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":["Content made by others for specific use in your entry (for example viewer comments or a graphic made at your request). Existing graphics and clips are fine.","Content hosted on an external website, or links to external websites, other than the software download link."],"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A website is a set of connected web pages that uses multimedia to communicate your historical argument, research and interpretation of your topic's significance in history. It has individual and group options.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Multimedia clips (audio, video or both) may total no more than 3 minutes. Music or songs that play after a page loads are counted in the 3 minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- The website must be an original production and show your ability to use website design software and technology. You need access to the internet and appropriate software and equipment.\\n- You must build the entry with the NHD website editor, NHDWebCentral (nhd.org/nhdwebcentral). You must run all software and equipment in developing the site. All pages must be connected by navigation links. The site is limited to 100MB of file space.\\n- You may use professional photographs, graphics, video, recorded music and similar, built into the website, credited in the site and cited in the annotated bibliography.\\n- Word limit: no more than 1,200 visible words that you write. Words that count: text you write, including captions, graphs, timelines and multimedia. Words that do not count: code and alternate text tags on images, required source credits, recurring menus, titles and navigation instructions, primary or secondary materials or quotes, the required home page items, and the annotated bibliography and process paper.\\n- Home page: one page must be the home page. It shows your name(s), the entry title, division and category, the number of visible student-written words, the total length of multimedia, the number of words in the process paper, and the main menu to the sections. It must not have the name of your teacher or your school.\\n- You may record quotes and primary source materials for dramatic effect, but you may not narrate your own compositions or other explanatory material.\\n- If multimedia needs special software to view (for example Flash, QuickTime or Real Player), give a link on the same page to a website where it is a free, secure and legal download.\\n- Judges try to view all multimedia, but files that cannot be viewed cannot be judged.\\n- All quotes and visual sources must be credited on the website and cited in the annotated bibliography. Remove the hyperlink from all URLs in the on-screen source credit. Source credits do not count toward the word total.\\n- The annotated bibliography and process paper must be part of the website, in PDF format and in the navigation. No printed copies are needed.\\n- Extensive supplemental material is not appropriate. Oral history transcripts, letters, questionnaires and other sources are cited in the bibliography, not added to the website.\\n- The content and look of a page cannot change when the page is refreshed. Random text or image generators are not allowed.\\n- The pages must be viewable in a recent version of a standard web browser (for example Microsoft Edge, Mozilla Firefox, Apple Safari, Google Chrome).\\n- Send the website URL to the Contest Coordinator by the deadline. The website is locked during judging.\\n\\n## Not allowed\\n- Content made by others for specific use in your entry (for example viewer comments or a graphic made at your request). Existing graphics and clips are fine.\\n- Content hosted on an external website, or links to external websites, other than the software download link."}]`),r=[],s=[{Key:"RuleBookEdition",Kind:"text",Label:"Contest Rule Book edition in force",Value:"June 22, 2020 edition (supersedes all earlier versions)",Source:"NHD Contest Rule Book (June 2020 edition) p.2; nhd.org Contest Rules page"},{Key:"AnnualTheme",Kind:"text",Label:"Annual theme for the 2027 contest",Value:"Innovation in History: Impact, Influence, Change",Source:"nhd.org Theme page"},{Key:"NationalContestDates",Kind:"date",Label:"National Contest (2027)",Value:"June 13-17, 2027",Source:"nhd.org National Contest page"},{Key:"NationalAwardsCeremony",Kind:"date",Label:"National Contest Awards Ceremony (2027)",Value:"Thursday, June 17, 2027, Xfinity Center, University of Maryland, College Park",Source:"nhd.org National Contest page"},{Key:"NationalVenue",Kind:"address",Label:"National Contest venue",Value:{Name:"University of Maryland, College Park",Address:"",City:"College Park, Maryland, USA",MapUrl:"",Directions:""},Source:"nhd.org National Contest page; NHD Contest Rule Book (June 2020 edition) p.3"},{Key:"LocalContestDates",Kind:"text",Label:"Local, regional and affiliate contest dates, deadlines and fees",Value:"Set by each Affiliate or Contest Coordinator. The Affiliate list at nhd.org/affiliates shows each contest date and place (for example April and May 2027 for many Affiliates).",Source:"NHD Contest Rule Book (June 2020 edition) p.8 (3.1, 3.2); nhd.org Find Your Local Contest page"},{Key:"AffiliateFinder",Kind:"link",Label:"Find your Affiliate and Contest Coordinator",Value:"https://nhd.org/affiliates",Source:"NHD Contest Rule Book (June 2020 edition) p.4 (nhd.org/affiliates)"},{Key:"NHDOffice",Kind:"address",Label:"National History Day office",Value:{Name:"National History Day",Address:"4511 Knox Road, Suite 205",City:"College Park, MD 20740",MapUrl:"",Directions:""},Source:"NHD Contest Rule Book (June 2020 edition) p.43"},{Key:"NHDContact",Kind:"contact",Label:"National History Day contact",Value:{Name:"National History Day",Role:"General questions about NHD rules and procedures",Phone:"(301) 314-9739",WhatsApp:"",Email:"info@nhd.org"},Source:"NHD Contest Rule Book (June 2020 edition) p.43"},{Key:"NHDWebsite",Kind:"link",Label:"National History Day website",Value:"https://nhd.org",Source:"NHD Contest Rule Book (June 2020 edition) p.43"},{Key:"Announcements",Kind:"news",Label:"Notices from nhd.org",Value:[{Date:"2025-11-21",Title:"2027 theme",Text:"The 2027 contest theme is Innovation in History: Impact, Influence, Change.",Url:"https://nhd.org/en/contest/theme/"},{Date:"",Title:"Judge registration",Text:"Judge registration for the 2027 National Contest opens in Fall 2026. The 2027 National Contest is in person at the University of Maryland, College Park.",Url:""}],Source:"nhd.org National Contest and Theme pages"}],l=[{Claim:"Contest rules for all categories, divisions, group rules, advancement, written materials, category rules and evaluation: NHD Contest Rule Book, June 22, 2020 edition (current edition per nhd.org).",SourceType:"Primary - the organiser's own rules document",Source:"NHD Contest Rule Book (June 2020 edition)",Url:"https://nhd.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/NHDRuleBook2020Digital.pdf"},{Claim:"The Contest Rules and Evaluation page confirms the June 22, 2020 Contest Rule Book is the current rule book and describes the two evaluation sections (Historical Quality 80 percent, Clarity of Presentation 20 percent).",SourceType:"Primary - the organiser's own website",Source:"nhd.org Contest Rules & Evaluation",Url:"https://nhd.org/en/contest/contest-rules/"},{Claim:"National Contest 2027 dates, venue and awards, and the 2027 theme.",SourceType:"Primary - the organiser's own website",Source:"nhd.org National Contest and Theme pages",Url:"https://nhd.org/en/contest/national-contest/"}],u={TemplateKey:e,Year:2026,Note:t,Page:o,ParticipantTypes:n,AgeGroups:a,Competitions:i,CompetitionAgeGroups:r,Variables:s,Evidence:l};export{a as AgeGroups,r as CompetitionAgeGroups,i as Competitions,l as Evidence,t as Note,o as Page,n as ParticipantTypes,e as TemplateKey,s as Variables,d as Year,u as default};
